
   !!! IMPORTANT !!!

   There are two ways in which we can "read" sources (configmap/secret): bootstrap, also called the legacy mode
   and "config data", the new mode. Config data, unlike bootstrap, does not require any bootstrap.yaml files,
   but it has one issue that makes its usage a bit complicated:

            https://github.com/spring-cloud/spring-cloud-kubernetes/issues/1173

    In simple words, users are basically "blind" and no logs are reported when using this mode.
    Imagine you have an app that needs to read some configmap, but the RBAC rules are incorrect.
    In this case, the logs are going to be "clean" and no error will be reported. The error still
    happens, but its not reported in logs.
    To inflict more pain:

     a) config data is the default mode that we currently use
     b) switching to the bootstrap mode where this issue does not happen, means code changes:
        you need to create another "bootstrap.yaml" and use an env variable (a property will not work)
        to enable this mode.

   As such, for this sample, I am going to use the "bootstrap" mode, though if properly configured
   from the beginning, config data will work as well. In my work projects (where we use spring-cloud-kubernetes),
   I usually "develop" with bootstrap and when I know all is good, switch to config data.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


 -  assumes you hava java-21
 -  running 'mvn clean install' should build the image
 -  you can check that via 'docker images | grep sample'

 -  create local cluster: 'kind create cluster'
 -  load the image into the cluster : 'kind load docker-image sample:latest'

 - create namespaces:
      "cd src/main/k8s"
      "kubectl apply -f 0_namespaces.yaml"

 -  create configmap:
      "kubectl apply -f 1_configmap.yaml"

 -  create rbac :
      "kubectl apply -f 2_rbac.yaml"

 - check that rbac is correctly set-up (only one check here)

       "kubectl auth can-i list services --as=system:serviceaccount:default:spring-cloud-kubernetes-configuration-watcher -n a"
           (you must get a 'yes')

       "kubectl auth can-i list services --as=system:serviceaccount:default:spring-cloud-kubernetes-configuration-watcher -n default"
           (you must get a 'yes')

       "kubectl auth can-i list services --as=system:serviceaccount:a:spring-cloud-kubernetes-configuration-watcher -n default"
           (you must get a 'yes')

        "kubectl auth can-i list services --as=system:serviceaccount:a:spring-cloud-kubernetes-configuration-watcher -n a"
           (you must get a 'yes')

 - create deployments:
      "kubectl apply -f 3_deployments.yaml"

 -  verify that the pods are up and running:

    exec into the cluster, by finding the ID of it first:
       "docker ps | grep kind | awk '{ print $1 }'"
       "docker exec -u 0 --privileged -it 2eec1b8eaab4 bash"

    find the IPs of the pods:
       "kubectl get pods -o wide --all-namespaces"

    with the 'IP' column above issue a curl to both pods:
       "curl 10.244.0.3:8080/actuator/health"

 - verify that configmap was correctly wired and properties from it are available:
       "curl 10.244.0.3:8080/value"
       (you should see the 'initial' response)

 - create services:
      "kubectl apply -f 4_services.yaml"

 - verify that pods are accessible via the created service (NOT mandatory by any means)
   I am going to create one more pod for that:
      "kubectl run wiremock --image=wiremock/wiremock:latest --command "sleep" -- 10000"

   Then run such a command to see that the service can be accessed:
      "kubectl exec wiremock -- curl http://sample-in-a.a.svc.cluster.local:8080/value"
      "kubectl exec wiremock -- curl http://sample-in-default.default.svc.cluster.local:8080/value"

 - check the logs of configuration watcher, you must see a line like this:
        added configmap informer for namespace : default with filter : spring.cloud.kubernetes.config.informer.enabled=true
        ( which means we will only be looking in namespace 'default' for config maps that have the label
         spring.cloud.kubernetes.config.informer.enabled=true )

 - change the configmap to a different value then 'initial' and do
       "kubectl replace -f 1_configmap.yaml"

 - wait for at least 5 seconds (refresh delay config) and issue the curls again against the
   pod IPs. The value is now going to be "changed".














